1
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:03,860
Hi, I'm Chris.

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:05,260
I've spent over 10 years

3
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:06,220
in data engineering

4
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:09,100
and interviewed more than 200 engineers.

5
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:10,860
Now I run Stackle, where I teach

6
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:13,020
the skills that actually get you hired.

7
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:14,560
Let's get into it.

8
00:00:15,097 --> 00:00:17,397
Every time you tap "order" in an app,

9
00:00:17,417 --> 00:00:19,557
that tap becomes an event.

10
00:00:19,577 --> 00:00:21,024
The payment service needs it.

11
00:00:21,044 --> 00:00:22,277
The warehouse needs it.

12
00:00:22,297 --> 00:00:23,637
Analytics needs it.

13
00:00:23,657 --> 00:00:25,477
All of them, right now.

14
00:00:25,497 --> 00:00:27,317
Wiring every service to every

15
00:00:27,337 --> 00:00:29,397
other one turns into spaghetti.

16
00:00:29,417 --> 00:00:30,997
So a lot of companies put

17
00:00:31,017 --> 00:00:32,677
Apache Kafka in the middle.

18
00:00:32,697 --> 00:00:34,037
Kafka is an open-source

19
00:00:34,057 --> 00:00:36,304
platform for streaming events.

20
00:00:36,324 --> 00:00:38,197
Streaming just means data flows the moment

21
00:00:38,217 --> 00:00:40,597
it happens, not in a nightly batch.

22
00:00:40,617 --> 00:00:42,277
It started at LinkedIn, and became

23
00:00:42,297 --> 00:00:44,917
a top-level Apache project in 2012.

24
00:00:44,937 --> 00:00:46,384
Here's the core idea.

25
00:00:46,404 --> 00:00:48,597
Producers write events to a topic.

26
00:00:48,617 --> 00:00:50,437
A topic is just a named log,

27
00:00:50,457 --> 00:00:51,797
like "orders".

28
00:00:51,817 --> 00:00:53,344
New events go on the end,

29
00:00:53,364 --> 00:00:54,317
and Kafka keeps them

30
00:00:54,337 --> 00:00:55,957
for as long as you choose.

31
00:00:55,977 --> 00:00:58,330
Consumers read that log at their own pace,

32
00:00:58,350 --> 00:00:59,317
and remember their place

33
00:00:59,337 --> 00:01:01,597
with a number called an offset.

34
00:01:01,617 --> 00:01:03,077
Now, the architecture.

35
00:01:03,097 --> 00:01:04,517
A Kafka cluster is a group

36
00:01:04,537 --> 00:01:06,277
of servers called brokers.

37
00:01:06,297 --> 00:01:08,437
Each topic is split into partitions,

38
00:01:08,457 --> 00:01:09,557
spread across the brokers,

39
00:01:09,577 --> 00:01:11,637
so many machines share the load.

40
00:01:11,657 --> 00:01:13,077
Every partition is copied

41
00:01:13,097 --> 00:01:13,957
to other brokers,

42
00:01:13,977 --> 00:01:16,517
so if one fails, the data is still there.

43
00:01:16,537 --> 00:01:18,357
Consumers join a consumer group,

44
00:01:18,377 --> 00:01:19,357
and each partition

45
00:01:19,377 --> 00:01:20,570
goes to one member,

46
00:01:20,590 --> 00:01:22,757
so you scale by adding consumers.

47
00:01:22,777 --> 00:01:24,917
And a few controllers, running KRaft,

48
00:01:24,937 --> 00:01:26,277
keep track of which broker

49
00:01:26,297 --> 00:01:27,797
leads each partition.

50
00:01:27,817 --> 00:01:30,397
So when a team says, "we use Kafka,"

51
00:01:30,417 --> 00:01:31,557
it means their services

52
00:01:31,577 --> 00:01:32,837
don't call each other.

53
00:01:32,857 --> 00:01:35,117
They publish events once, and anyone who

54
00:01:35,137 --> 00:01:37,477
needs them can read them, as they happen.

55
00:01:37,497 --> 00:01:39,337
That's Apache Kafka.

56
00:01:41,397 --> 00:01:43,537
So, what should I explain next?

57
00:01:43,557 --> 00:01:44,697
Tell me in the comments below,

58
00:01:44,717 --> 00:01:46,917
and it could be the next episode.
